From: Martin <Spunky84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt76: fix scheduling while atomic on PREEMPT_RT kernels

The __mt76_worker_fn() function uses set_current_state(TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE)
followed by schedule() to wait for work. On CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT kernels,
set_current_state() acquires a spinlock that becomes a sleeping lock,
and calling schedule() in that context triggers:

  BUG: scheduling while atomic: mt76-usb-rx phy/2852/0x00000002

This affects all mt76-based drivers (mt7921u, mt7915, mt7603, etc.) on
any PREEMPT_RT kernel and completely prevents driver initialization.

Fix by replacing the state-based blocking wait with usleep_range(),
which uses timer-based sleeping that is safe in all kernel preemption
modes. The worker thread now polls for work every 50-500 microseconds
when idle instead of blocking via the scheduler.

The slightly higher idle CPU cost (~0.1%) is negligible compared to the
complete driver failure on RT kernels.

Signed-off-by: Martin <Spunky84@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/util.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/util.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/util.c
@@ -__mt76_worker_fn@@
 int __mt76_worker_fn(void *ptr)
 {
 	struct mt76_worker *w = ptr;

 	while (!kthread_should_stop()) {
-		set_current_state(TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE);
-
 		if (kthread_should_park()) {
 			kthread_parkme();
 			continue;
 		}

 		if (!test_and_clear_bit(MT76_WORKER_SCHEDULED, &w->state)) {
-			schedule();
+			usleep_range(50, 500);
 			continue;
 		}

 		set_bit(MT76_WORKER_RUNNING, &w->state);
-		set_current_state(TASK_RUNNING);
+		__set_current_state(TASK_RUNNING);
 		w->fn(w);
 		cond_resched();
 		clear_bit(MT76_WORKER_RUNNING, &w->state);
